    \u043e\u043f\u0438\u0441\u044b\u0432\u0430\u0442\u044c \u0440\u0435\u0430\u043b\u044c\u043d\u043e\u0441\u0442\u044c
    \u043d\u0430 \u044f\u0437\u044b\u043a\u0435 \u0447\u0438\u0441\u0435\u043b."
  interestingFactsEn: "Jean-Baptiste Joseph Furier's life is full of sudden twists and curious details that go far beyond his famous mathematical formulas.\n\nThe \"Fire\" obsession and unusual death of Furier was known for his literal obsession with heat, the very matter he studied. Contemporarys said that he was constantly cold and compensated for it in extravagant ways.There's even a half-mythical story that his doctors linked health problems, and eventually an aneurysm, which caused death in 1830, to this habit of severe overheating.\n\n2. \"Dance with the Emperor\": The Fourier's dramatic political game, a loyal bonapartist, held the post of Prefect of the Department of Izer. When Napoleon went on the first link to the Elbe in 1814, his journey was through Grenoble. Fearing the meeting and compromise in front of the new authorities, Furje changed the Emperor's route by tricks, avoiding the meeting. Napoleon triumphantly returned to France in 1815 (known as \"One Days\") and the situation was repeated.This time, in order to preserve the loyalty of the Royalists, Furier prepared a greeting for the Emperor, wrote an explanatory note about his conflict of interest and... in panic, ran out of town, and the angry Napoleon demanded his removal, but when his friends showed the Emperor \"Historical Introduction\" to \"The Description of Egypt,\" which was inspired by his genius, anger changed to mercy.Not only did Napoleon forgive Fourier and appoint him Prefect of the Ron Department, an even higher post, but this position lasted only about two months, until Bonaparte was finally defeated in Waterloo.\n\n3. The night classes in the light of the Tug's garrets for knowledge in Furier have been evident since his early years. At the age of 13, he began to engage in mathematics, and at the age of 14, he had already mastered the entire six-volume course of mathematics, which was written by Etienne Bezu. His passion was so great that he, as a student of the military school under the monastery, collected his candlelights to keep him up at night while everyone else was asleep.\n\n4. The birth of the greenhouse effect and the CT scans of Fourier is not only a mathematician, but also one of the pioneers of the greenhouse effect mechanism. By studying the Earth's thermal balance, he realized that the atmosphere acts as a heat trap, like a glass in a greenhouse that misses sunlight but delays heat. He published his findings in 1827.He is also the author of the thermal conductivity equation, which describes how heat is spreading through the bodies. Today this equation is the key to creating images in MRIs and CT scanners.\n\n5. The teacher who raised the genius of Fourier was not only an outstanding scientist, but also a talented teacher, and as a professor of Polytechnics, he raised a whole generation of brilliant mathematicians, including Peter Gustave Dirichle and Claude Louis Navier, and it was Navier who completed and published his unfinished work on algebra after the death of Furier.\n\n6. The Egyptian scientist and construction worker of Napoleon &apos; s scientific expedition to Egypt Fourier was not just a passive observer, but he was appointed secretary of the Cairo Institute and ran archaeological excavations; he was also actively involved in administrative work, setting up weapons and gunpowder for the army; upon return, he was charged with editing the huge scientific work \"The Description of Egypt\".The name Furier is permanently on the Eiffel Tower among the 72 greatest scientists in France. In the history of science, he has remained not just a mathematician, but a man who has turned physics into an accurate science that can describe reality in the language of numbers."
